  "text": "Long‑term flow of water that is low in dissolved minerals over carbonate‑rich material that still contains leftover hydrogen sulfide leads to biologically driven reduction of sulfate and a buildup of secondary hydrogen sulfide. The water from layers with varying mineral content contains enough sulfate, phosphate and ammonia‑derived nitrogen to support active sulfate‑reducing microbes. In zones where the water is more diluted, the rocks lose sulfate and phosphate, while the concentrations of dissolved carbon dioxide and its bicarbonate form rise, reaching values around two hundred milligrams per litre for CO₂ and seven hundred milligrams per litre for bicarbonate. The peak sulfate concentration observed was on the order of a few hundred milligrams per litre, and phosphate peaked near three‑tenths of a milligram per litre. Ammonium levels fell as mineralization increased, staying between roughly 130 and 7 mg/L. The densest populations of sulfate‑reducing bacteria—tens of thousands of cells per millilitre—were detected in water with a total mineral content of about 19 g/L. All measured parameters showed their highest values in a highly saline deposit with about 62 g/L total dissolved solids."
